  "title": "NAS-5GS: Fix URSP Destination FQDN off-by-one decoding",
  "description": "## Summary\n\nFix incorrect decoding of the Destination FQDN traffic descriptor component (type 0x91) in URSP rules. The first character of the FQDN was being dropped.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe Destination FQDN value is decoded using `ENC_APN_STR`, which follows RFC 1035 DNS label encoding — it interprets the first byte as a label length and skips it. This causes the displayed FQDN to lose its first character.\n\nExample with encoded hex `91 0B 6578616D706C652E636F6D`:\n- Type: `0x91` (Destination FQDN)\n- Length: `0x0B` (11 bytes)\n- Value: `6578616D706C652E636F6D` = \"example.com\" (plain ASCII)\n\nCurrent display: `Destination FQDN: xample.com` (first byte `0x65` = 'e' misinterpreted as label length 101)\n\n## Changes\n\n- Changed encoding from `ENC_APN_STR` to `ENC_UTF_8` for the Destination FQDN field (1 line change)\n\n## Specification Reference\n\n3GPP TS 24.526 Section 5.2:\n\n> For \"destination FQDN type\", the traffic descriptor component value field shall be encoded as a sequence of one octet destination FQDN length field and a destination FQDN value of variable size.\n\nWhile the spec text references RFC 1035, the actual encoding in URSP implementations uses a plain UTF-8 character string (dot-separated, not DNS label format). This is consistent with how other URSP string-type descriptors (DNN, Regular expression) are encoded in the same specification.\n\n## Testing\n\nVerified with pcap file containing Destination FQDN \"example.com\":\n\n- **Before:** `Destination FQDN: xample.com`\n- **After:** `Destination FQDN: example.com`\n\nReproduction pcap: [TD_0x91_dest_FQDN.pcap](https://gitlab.com/-/project/82634231/uploads/f11158b308f5c01fe6220b021226613f/TD_0x91_dest_FQDN.pcap)",
